# AutoWorkshop AI — backup schedule for the production Docker host (T-0018).
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp autoworkshop-backup.cron /etc/cron.d/autoworkshop-backup
#   sudo chown root:root /etc/cron.d/autoworkshop-backup
#   sudo chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/autoworkshop-backup
#
# `/etc/cron.d` rather than a user crontab because it is a file in the repo:
# reviewable, diffable, and reinstalled by the same IaC that rebuilds the host.
# A schedule that exists only in one machine's crontab is lost with that machine
# — which is precisely the disaster this whole subsystem is defending against.
#
# The user must be able to run `docker` (in the `docker` group). Set
# AW_HOME to wherever the repository is checked out on the host.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
AW_HOME=/opt/autoworkshop-ai

# Health check every 6 hours. Cheap, and it is the thing that notices a schedule
# has silently stopped firing — the failure mode that hid the WAL archiving
# defect for five hours. Non-zero exit makes cron mail the output.
0 */6 * * *   autoworkshop  $AW_HOME/infrastructure/backup/run-scheduled.sh health

# Daily backup at 02:15. Off-peak, and offset from the hour so it does not
# collide with everything else on the host that runs at :00.
15 2 * * *    autoworkshop  $AW_HOME/infrastructure/backup/run-scheduled.sh daily

# Weekly full backup, Sunday 03:15. Same artefacts, different retention label.
15 3 * * 0    autoworkshop  $AW_HOME/infrastructure/backup/run-scheduled.sh weekly

# Monthly restore drill, 1st of the month at 04:15 (`1.txt` §37).
# This is the one that matters. Everything above produces files; only this
# proves they are worth having.
15 4 1 * *    autoworkshop  $AW_HOME/infrastructure/backup/run-scheduled.sh drill
